<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Exercise for the Lagrange Multipliers section, collected in the Exercises chapter.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="exercises-lagrange">
  <title> Exercises for <xref ref="section-lagrange-multipliers" text="title"/></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      If you would like to review this topic before attempting the exercises,
      click <xref ref="section-lagrange-multipliers" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-parallel-tangent-plane">
      <title>Tangent Planes Parallel to a Given Plane</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the points on the surface
          <me>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</me>
          at which the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>-x - 5y - 2z = -3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Multiplying by <m>-1</m>, the given plane is
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>, with normal vector
          <m>\mathbf{n} = \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle</m>.
          The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is parallel to this plane exactly
          at the points of the ellipsoid that are closest to and farthest from
          it, so we minimize and maximize the distance to the plane subject to
          the constraint of lying on the ellipsoid.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-schematic">
          <caption>
            A schematic cross-section.  The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is
            parallel to the given plane exactly at the closest point <m>S_1</m>
            and the farthest point <m>S_2</m>, where <m>\nabla g</m> is
            parallel to the plane's normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-lagrange-schematic" width="75%">
            <prefigure label="lagrange-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(500,330)" margins="5">
                <definition>S1 = (sqrt(2), sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>S2 = (-sqrt(2), -sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>P = (2.5, 1)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="[-3.6,-2.3,5.6,3.3]">

                  <group at="constraint">
                    <ellipse at="ellipse" center="(0,0)" axes="(2,1)"
                             stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="constraint-label" anchor="(0.4,-1.12)" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>g(x,y,z)=0</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="plane">
                    <line at="plane-line" p1="(4.9,-0.2)" p2="(-0.1,2.3)"
                          stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="plane-label" anchor="(0.8,2.35)" alignment="north">
                      <m>x+5y+2z=3</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="tangencies">
                    <group at="tangency-S1">
                      <line at="tangent-S1" p1="S1" p2="S1+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S1" v="(0.65,1.3)" tail="S1"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S1-label" anchor="S1+(0.65,1.3)" alignment="west">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S1-point" p="S1" fill="red" alignment="southwest">
                        <m>S_1</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                    <group at="tangency-S2">
                      <line at="tangent-S2" p1="S2" p2="S2+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S2" v="(-0.65,-1.3)" tail="S2"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S2-label" anchor="S2+(-0.65,-1.3)" alignment="southeast">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S2-point" p="S2" fill="red" alignment="northeast">
                        <m>S_2</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                  </group>

                  <group at="distance">
                    <line at="distance-segment" p1="S1" p2="P"
                          stroke="red" dash="4 4" thickness="2"/>
                  </group>

                  <group at="normal">
                    <vector at="n-vector" v="(0.55,1.1)" tail="P"
                            stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="n-label" anchor="P+(0.55,1.1)" alignment="northeast">
                      <m>\mathbf{n}=\langle 1,5,2\rangle</m>
                    </label>
                    <point at="P-point" p="P" fill="black" alignment="east">
                      <m>P(3,0,0)</m>
                    </point>
                  </group>

                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="A schematic cross-section of the ellipsoid together with the given plane, the two tangency points, and the gradient vectors">
                    <annotation ref="constraint"
                                text="The constraint curve g equals 0, a cross-section of the ellipsoid x squared plus y squared plus 3 z squared equals 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="plane"
                                text="The given plane x plus 5 y plus 2 z equals 3, which does not meet the ellipsoid"/>
                    <annotation ref="tangencies"
                                text="The two points on the ellipsoid where the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane">
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S1"
                                  text="At S 1, the point of the ellipsoid closest to the plane, the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g is parallel to n"/>
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S2"
                                  text="At S 2, the point of the ellipsoid farthest from the plane, the tangent plane is again parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g points opposite to n"/>
                    </annotation>
                    <annotation ref="distance"
                                text="The distance from S 1 to the plane, the quantity minimized in the Lagrange multiplier method"/>
                    <annotation ref="normal"
                                text="The point P equals 3 comma 0 comma 0 chosen on the plane, with the normal vector n equals 1 comma 5 comma 2"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>

              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Choose a point on the plane, say <m>P(3,0,0)</m>.
          For a point <m>S(x,y,z)</m> on the ellipsoid, the distance from
          <m>S</m> to the plane is
          <me>
            \frac{\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\mathbf{n}}{\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}
            = \frac{\langle x-3,\, y,\, z\rangle\cdot\langle 1,5,2\rangle}
                   {\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}.
          </me>
          Since <m>\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert</m> is constant, it suffices to
          optimize the numerator
          <me>
            f(x,y,z) = x - 3 + 5y + 2z
          </me>
          subject to the constraint
          <me>
            g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 - 1 = 0.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla f = \lambda\, \nabla g</m> gives
          <me>
            \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle = \lambda \langle 2x,\, 2y,\, 6z\rangle,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            x = \frac{1}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            y = \frac{5}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            z = \frac{1}{3\lambda}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting into the constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{25}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{3}{9\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{3 + 75 + 4}{12\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{\lambda^2} \amp= \frac{12}{82},</mrow>
          </md>
          so that
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\lambda} = \pm 2\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            x = \pm\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            y = \pm 5\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            z = \pm\sqrt{\frac{2}{123}},
          </me>
          and the two points are
          <md>
            <mrow>S_1\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; 5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; \sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right)
            \amp \qquad\text{and}</mrow>
            <mrow>S_2\!\left(-\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right). \amp</mrow>
          </md>
          The positive signs give <m>S_1</m>, the point closest to the plane,
          and the negative signs give <m>S_2</m>, the farthest.  At both
          points the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <exercise xml:id="exer-xy-on-circle">
      <title>A product on a circle</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the maximum and minimum values of the function
          <m>f(x,y) = 4xy</m> subject to the constraint
          <m>x^2 + y^2 = 8</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Write the constraint as
          <me>
            g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 8 = 0,
          </me>
          so that <m>g_x = 2x</m> and <m>g_y = 2y</m>, while
          <m>f_x = 4y</m> and <m>f_y = 4x</m>.
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g</m> gives
          <me>
            4y = 2\lambda x, \qquad 4x = 2\lambda y.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Solving each equation for <m>\lambda</m>,
          <me>
            \lambda = \frac{2y}{x} = \frac{2x}{y}
            \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
            x^2 = y^2.
          </me>
          Substituting into the constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 - 8 = 0</m> gives
          <m>2x^2 = 8</m>, so
          <me>
            x = \pm 2, \qquad y = \pm 2.
          </me>
          Hence there are four extrema:
          <m>(2,2)</m>, <m>(2,-2)</m>, <m>(-2,2)</m>, <m>(-2,-2)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Evaluating <m>f</m> at the four candidates:
        </p>

        <tabular halign="center">
          <col width="30%"/>
          <col width="30%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>(x,y)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f(x,y)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(2,2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>16</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(-2,-2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>16</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(2,-2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-16</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(-2,2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-16</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          The maximum value is <m>16</m>, attained at <m>(2,2)</m> and
          <m>(-2,-2)</m>; the minimum value is <m>-16</m>, attained at
          <m>(2,-2)</m> and <m>(-2,2)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Geometric interpretation.</em>  A rectangle with corners
          <m>(\pm x, \pm y)</m> inscribed in the circle <m>x^2+y^2=8</m>
          has area exactly <m>4xy = f(x,y)</m>.  As the corner
          <m>(x,y)</m> moves along the circle, we obtain rectangles of
          different areas.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-inscribed-rects">
          <caption>
            Some of the possible rectangles inscribed in the circle
            <m>x^2+y^2=8</m>.  Each has corners <m>(\pm x, \pm y)</m> for a
            point <m>(x,y)</m> on the circle, and therefore area
            <m>4xy = f(x,y)</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-inscribed-rects" width="55%">
            <prefigure label="inscribed-rects" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,360)" margins="5">
                <definition>r = sqrt(8)</definition>
                <definition>A = (r*cos(pi/9), r*sin(pi/9))</definition>
                <definition>B = (r*cos(11*pi/36), r*sin(11*pi/36))</definition>
                <definition>C = (r*cos(7*pi/18), r*sin(7*pi/18))</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="[-4.4,-3.9,4.4,3.9]">

                  <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

                  <group at="constraint">
                    <circle at="circle" center="(0,0)" radius="sqrt(8)"
                            stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="circle-label" anchor="(1.1,-2.95)" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>x^2+y^2=8</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="candidates">
                    <polygon at="rect-A" points="(A, (-A[0],A[1]), -A, (A[0],-A[1]))"
                             closed="yes" stroke="gray" thickness="2" dash="7 4"/>
                    <polygon at="rect-B" points="(B, (-B[0],B[1]), -B, (B[0],-B[1]))"
                             closed="yes" stroke="orange" thickness="2" dash="7 4"/>
                    <polygon at="rect-C" points="(C, (-C[0],C[1]), -C, (C[0],-C[1]))"
                             closed="yes" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="7 4"/>
                    <point at="corner-generic" p="B" fill="orange" alignment="northeast">
                      <m>(x,y)</m>
                    </point>
                  </group>

                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="The circle x squared plus y squared equals 8 with several inscribed rectangles of different proportions">
                    <annotation ref="constraint"
                                text="The constraint curve, the circle x squared plus y squared equals 8"/>
                    <annotation ref="candidates"
                                text="Three of the possible inscribed rectangles, each with corners plus or minus x comma plus or minus y and area 4 x y">
                      <annotation ref="rect-A"
                                  text="A wide and short inscribed rectangle"/>
                      <annotation ref="rect-B"
                                  text="An inscribed rectangle of intermediate proportions, with the generic corner x comma y marked on the circle"/>
                      <annotation ref="rect-C"
                                  text="A tall and narrow inscribed rectangle"/>
                    </annotation>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>

              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The values of <m>(x,y)</m> that maximize <m>f</m> are exactly the
          values that maximize the area of the inscribed rectangle.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-xy-on-circle">
          <caption>
            The optimal solution: the inscribed rectangle with corners
            <m>(\pm 2, \pm 2)</m>, whose area
            <m>4xy = f(2,2) = 16</m> is the largest possible.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-xy-on-circle" width="55%">
            <prefigure label="xy-on-circle" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,360)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="[-4.4,-3.9,4.4,3.9]">

                  <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

                  <group at="constraint">
                    <circle at="circle" center="(0,0)" radius="sqrt(8)"
                            stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="circle-label" anchor="(1.1,-2.95)" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>x^2+y^2=8</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="rectangle">
                    <polygon at="rect" points="((2,2),(-2,2),(-2,-2),(2,-2))"
                             closed="yes" stroke="red" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <label at="area-label" anchor="(0,-1.1)" alignment="center"
                           clear-background="yes">
                      <m>\text{Area} = 4xy</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="corners">
                    <point at="corner-pp" p="(2,2)" fill="red" alignment="northeast">
                      <m>(2,2)</m>
                    </point>
                    <point at="corner-mp" p="(-2,2)" fill="red" alignment="northwest">
                      <m>(-2,2)</m>
                    </point>
                    <point at="corner-mm" p="(-2,-2)" fill="red" alignment="southwest">
                      <m>(-2,-2)</m>
                    </point>
                    <point at="corner-pm" p="(2,-2)" fill="red" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>(2,-2)</m>
                    </point>
                  </group>

                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="The circle x squared plus y squared equals 8 with an inscribed rectangle whose corners are the four constrained extrema">
                    <annotation ref="constraint"
                                text="The constraint curve, the circle x squared plus y squared equals 8"/>
                    <annotation ref="rectangle"
                                text="The inscribed rectangle with corners plus or minus 2 comma plus or minus 2, whose area is 4 x y"/>
                    <annotation ref="corners"
                                text="The four constrained extrema of f">
                      <annotation ref="corner-pp"
                                  text="The point 2 comma 2, where f attains its maximum value 16"/>
                      <annotation ref="corner-mm"
                                  text="The point minus 2 comma minus 2, where f also attains its maximum value 16"/>
                      <annotation ref="corner-pm"
                                  text="The point 2 comma minus 2, where f attains its minimum value minus 16"/>
                      <annotation ref="corner-mp"
                                  text="The point minus 2 comma 2, where f also attains its minimum value minus 16"/>
                    </annotation>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>

              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <exercise xml:id="exer-cobb-douglas">
      <title>Maximizing a Cobb-Douglas utility</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A consumer has <dollar/>600 to spend on two commodities, the first
          of which costs <dollar/>20 per unit and the second <dollar/>30 per
          unit.  Suppose that the utility derived by the consumer from
          <m>x</m> units of the first commodity and <m>y</m> units of the
          second commodity is given by the Cobb-Douglas utility function
          <me>
            U(x,y) = 10\,x^{0.6}y^{0.4}.
          </me>
          How many units of each commodity should the consumer buy to
          maximize utility?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The budget constraint is <m>20x + 30y = 600</m>, so we take
          <me>
            g(x,y) = 20x + 30y - 600 = 0,
          </me>
          with <m>g_x = 20</m> and <m>g_y = 30</m>, and maximize
          <m>U(x,y) = 10\,x^{0.6}y^{0.4}</m>, whose partial derivatives are
          <me>
            U_x = 6\,x^{-0.4}y^{0.4}, \qquad
            U_y = 4\,x^{0.6}y^{-0.6}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla U = \lambda\,\nabla g</m> gives
          the system
          <me>
            6\,x^{-0.4}y^{0.4} = 20\lambda, \qquad
            4\,x^{0.6}y^{-0.6} = 30\lambda.
          </me>
          Dividing the first equation by the second eliminates
          <m>\lambda</m>:
          <me>
            \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{y}{x} = \frac{2}{3}
            \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
            y = \frac{4}{9}\,x.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting into the budget constraint,
          <md>
            <mrow>20x + 30\left(\frac{4}{9}x\right) \amp= 600</mrow>
            <mrow>20x + \frac{40}{3}x \amp= 600</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{100}{3}x \amp= 600,</mrow>
          </md>
          so <m>x = 18</m> and <m>y = 18\cdot\frac{4}{9} = 8</m>.
          The consumer should buy <m>18</m> units of the first commodity and
          <m>8</m> units of the second.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <exercise xml:id="exer-jewelry-box">
      <title>Minimizing the cost of a box</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A jewelry box is to be constructed of material that costs
          <dollar/>1 per square inch for the bottom, <dollar/>2 per square
          inch for the sides, and <dollar/>5 per square inch for the top.
          If the total volume is to be <m>96 \text{ in}^3</m>, what
          dimensions will minimize the total cost of construction?
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-jewelry-box">
          <caption>
            The box with dimensions <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>: bottom and
            top of area <m>xy</m>, and sides of areas <m>xz</m> and
            <m>yz</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-jewelry-box" width="45%">
            <description>
              A rectangular box drawn in three dimensions, with the
              horizontal edges of the base labeled x and y and the vertical
              edge labeled z.
            </description>
            <latex-image label="jewelry-box">
              \begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, scale=1.0]
                \def\W{4}   % x : front width
                \def\H{2.5} % z : height
                \def\OX{1.5}\def\OY{0.85} % oblique offsets for the y depth
                % faces
                \fill[orange!30] (0,0) rectangle (\W,\H);
                \fill[orange!50]
                  (0,\H) -- (\OX,\H+\OY) -- (\W+\OX,\H+\OY) -- (\W,\H) -- cycle;
                \fill[orange!40]
                  (\W,0) -- (\W+\OX,\OY) -- (\W+\OX,\H+\OY) -- (\W,\H) -- cycle;
                % hidden edges
                \draw[orange!60!black, dashed]
                  (0,0) -- (\OX,\OY) -- (\W+\OX,\OY);
                \draw[orange!60!black, dashed] (\OX,\OY) -- (\OX,\H+\OY);
                % visible edges
                \draw[orange!60!black, thick] (0,0) rectangle (\W,\H);
                \draw[orange!60!black, thick]
                  (0,\H) -- (\OX,\H+\OY) -- (\W+\OX,\H+\OY) -- (\W,\H);
                \draw[orange!60!black, thick]
                  (\W,0) -- (\W+\OX,\OY) -- (\W+\OX,\H+\OY);
                % dimension labels
                \node[below] at (\W/2, 0) {$x$};
                \node[below right] at (\W + 0.5*\OX, 0.5*\OY) {$y$};
                \node[right] at (\W+\OX, \OY + 0.5*\H) {$z$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> be the dimensions of the base and
          <m>z</m> the height.  The bottom costs <m>1\cdot xy</m>, the top
          costs <m>5\cdot xy</m>, and the four sides cost
          <m>2(2xz) + 2(2yz)</m>, so the total cost of construction is
          <me>
            C(x,y,z) = 6xy + 4xz + 4yz,
          </me>
          with partial derivatives
          <me>
            C_x = 6y + 4z, \qquad
            C_y = 6x + 4z, \qquad
            C_z = 4x + 4y.
          </me>
          The volume requirement gives the constraint
          <me>
            g(x,y,z) = xyz - 96 = 0,
          </me>
          with <m>g_x = yz</m>, <m>g_y = xz</m>, <m>g_z = xy</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla C = \lambda\,\nabla g</m> gives
          the system
          <me>
            6y + 4z = \lambda yz, \qquad
            6x + 4z = \lambda xz, \qquad
            4x + 4y = \lambda xy.
          </me>
          Multiplying the equations by <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>
          respectively makes each right-hand side equal to
          <m>\lambda xyz</m>:
          <me>
            6xy + 4xz = 6xy + 4yz = 4xz + 4yz.
          </me>
          The first equality gives <m>4xz = 4yz</m>, so <m>x = y</m>.
          The second gives <m>6xy = 4xz</m>, so <m>z = \frac{3}{2}y</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting <m>y = x</m> and <m>z = \frac{3}{2}x</m> into the
          constraint <m>xyz = 96</m>,
          <me>
            (x)(x)\left(\tfrac{3}{2}x\right) = 96
            \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
            x^3 = 64,
          </me>
          so <m>x = 4</m>.  The cost is minimized by the dimensions
          <me>
            x = 4, \qquad y = 4, \qquad z = 6.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lm-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = xy</m> and <m>x^2 - y = 12</m>. We assume
<m>y \le 0</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Why do we need <m>y \le 0</m> here?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the extreme values of <m>f</m> subject to the above constraints.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Else a minimum and maximum would not exist, since we could just pick
<m>y</m> very large and <m>x = \pm\sqrt{y + 12}</m> would also have a
very large magnitude, resulting in a very large or very negative value
of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The constraint describes the portion of the parabola
<m>y = x^2 - 12</m> in the third and fourth quadrant. This shape is
closed and bounded so we are guaranteed the existence of global
maxima and minima. The two boundary points, which occur at
<m>y = 0</m>, need to be handled separately; this part is not very
hard, since we immediately see that <m>f</m> has value <m>0</m> on
them.
</p>
<p>
Now we proceed to find the critical points of <m>f</m> on the
parabola <m>g(x,y) = x^2 - y - 12 = 0</m>. The system of equations we
need to solve is obtained from
<m>\nabla f(x,y) = \langle y, x\rangle</m> and
<m>\nabla g(x,y) = \langle 2x, -1\rangle</m>. It is
<md>
<mrow>y \amp = 2\lambda x</mrow>
<mrow>x \amp = -\lambda</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 - y \amp = 12</mrow>
</md>
Combining the first two equations we see that <m>y = -2x^2</m>, and
using the third, <m>3x^2 = 12</m>, so <m>x = \pm 2</m> and
<m>y = -8</m>. Computing the value of <m>f</m> at these points, we
see that the global maximum of <m>f</m> is at <m>(-2, -8)</m> with a
value of <m>16</m>, while the global minimum with value <m>-16</m> is
at <m>(2, -8)</m>. See <xref ref="fig-rm2-lagrange"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rm2-lagrange">
<caption>
The constraint curve <m>y = x^2 - 12</m> with <m>y \le 0</m>, and the
level curves <m>xy = 16</m> and <m>xy = -16</m> of <m>f</m>. The
extrema occur at <m>(-2,-8)</m> and <m>(2,-8)</m>, where the level
curves are tangent to the parabola.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rm2-lagrange">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="12">
<definition>par(t) = (t, t^2 - 12)</definition>
<definition>hypp(t) = (t, 16/t)</definition>
<definition>hypm(t) = (t, -16/t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.2,-13.5,5.2,1.8)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="lm-par" function="par" domain="(-3.4641,3.4641)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="lm-hm" function="hypm" domain="(1.25,5.0)"
                  stroke="#009E73" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="lm-hp" function="hypp" domain="(-5.0,-1.25)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="lm-max" p="(-2,-8)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="lm-min" p="(2,-8)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="lm-par-lab" anchor="(3.0,-11.9)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=x^2-12</m>
</label>
<label at="lm-max-lab" anchor="(-2.15,-8.2)" alignment="sw" color="#D55E00">
<m>(-2,-8)</m>
</label>
<label at="lm-min-lab" anchor="(2.15,-8.2)" alignment="se" color="#009E73">
<m>(2,-8)</m>
</label>
<label at="lm-hp-lab" anchor="(-4.95,-2.6)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>xy=16</m>
</label>
<label at="lm-hm-lab" anchor="(5.0,-2.3)" alignment="sw" color="#009E73">
<m>xy=-16</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue parabola y equals x squared minus twelve, drawn for y less than or equal to zero, opens upward with vertex at zero comma negative twelve. A dashed vermillion hyperbola branch x y equals sixteen in the third quadrant touches the parabola at negative two comma negative eight, and a dashed green hyperbola branch x y equals negative sixteen in the fourth quadrant touches it at two comma negative eight.">
  <annotation ref="lm-par" text="The constraint curve, the portion of the parabola y equals x squared minus twelve with y at most zero."/>
  <annotation ref="lm-hp" text="The level curve x y equals sixteen, tangent to the parabola at the maximum point negative two comma negative eight."/>
  <annotation ref="lm-hm" text="The level curve x y equals negative sixteen, tangent to the parabola at the minimum point two comma negative eight."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-7">
<statement>
<p>
You have 24 square inches of cardboard and want to build a box in the
shape of a rectangular prism. Find the dimensions of the box, if you
would like it to have the largest possible volume. See
<xref ref="fig-rf-box"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rf-box">
<caption>
A rectangular prism with dimensions <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>.
</caption>
<image width="46%">
<description>
<p>
A rectangular box drawn in perspective, with the width labelled x on
the front left edge, the depth labelled y along the top front edge,
and the height labelled z on the left face.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % back edges
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0);
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (1.8,0,0);
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1.6);
  % visible edges
  \draw[thick] (1.8,0,0) -- (1.8,3.4,0) -- (0,3.4,0) -- (0,3.4,1.6) -- (0,0,1.6) -- (1.8,0,1.6) -- cycle;
  \draw[thick] (1.8,3.4,0) -- (1.8,3.4,1.6);
  \draw[thick] (1.8,0,1.6) -- (1.8,3.4,1.6) -- (0,3.4,1.6);
  % labels
  \node[below left=1pt] at (0.9,0,0) {$x$};
  \node[above=1pt] at (1.8,1.7,1.6) {$y$};
  \node[left=2pt] at (0,0,0.8) {$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The surface area of the box is
<md>
2xy + 2xz + 2yz = 24,
</md>
so the constraint is <m>g(x,y,z) = xy + xz + yz - 12 = 0</m>, and the
function to be maximized is <m>f(x,y,z) = xyz</m>. We compute
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = \langle f_x, f_y, f_z\rangle
= \langle yz, xz, xy\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla g \amp = \langle y+z, x+z, x+y\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
The Lagrange system <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>yz \amp = \lambda(y+z)</mrow>
<mrow>xz \amp = \lambda(x+z)</mrow>
<mrow>xy \amp = \lambda(x+y)</mrow>
</md>
Multiplying the equations by <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>
respectively gives
<md>
xyz = \lambda x(y+z), \qquad
xyz = \lambda y(x+z), \qquad
xyz = \lambda z(x+y).
</md>
Equating the first two, <m>x(y+z) = y(x+z)</m>, which simplifies to
<m>xz = yz</m>, i.e. <m>x = y</m>. Equating the last two,
<m>y(x+z) = z(x+y)</m>, which simplifies to <m>xy = xz</m>, i.e.
<m>y = z</m>.
</p>

<p>
So <m>x = y = z</m>, and substituting into the constraint
<m>xy + xz + yz - 12 = 0</m> gives <m>3x^2 = 12</m>, or
<m>x^2 = 4</m>. Hence
<md>
x = y = z = 2 \text{ inches}
</md>
and these dimensions (a cube) will maximize the volume.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find the point(s) on the surface <m>xyz = 1</m> closest to the origin.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The distance of a point from the origin is
<m>\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}</m>, so it suffices to minimize
<m>f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m> with the constraint
<m>g(x,y,z) = xyz - 1 = 0</m>. The Lagrange system
<m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
\langle 2x, 2y, 2z\rangle
= \langle \lambda yz, \lambda xz, \lambda xy\rangle.
</md>
Multiplying the first equation by <m>x</m> and using
<m>xyz = 1</m>,
<md>
2x = \lambda yz
\;\Rightarrow\; 2x^2 = \lambda xyz
\;\overset{xyz=1}{\Rightarrow}\; 2x^2 = \lambda
\;\Rightarrow\; x^2 = \frac{\lambda}{2},
</md>
and similarly <m>y^2 = \dfrac{\lambda}{2}</m> and
<m>z^2 = \dfrac{\lambda}{2}</m>, hence <m>x^2 = y^2 = z^2</m>.
</p>

<p>
Substituting into the constraint,
<md>
x^2 y^2 z^2 = 1
\;\Rightarrow\; x^6 = 1
\;\Rightarrow\; x^2 = 1.
</md>
Since <m>xyz = 1</m>, we know that <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>
are either all positive or two are negative and the third one is
positive. Hence, the closest points are
<md>
(1,1,1), \quad (1,-1,-1), \quad (-1,1,-1), \quad (-1,-1,1).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-10">
<statement>
<p>
Use the method of Lagrange Multipliers to find the maximum and
minimum values of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt2\,x - \sqrt2\,y</m> subject to the
constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 4 = 0</m>, we have
<md>
\nabla f = \sqrt2\,\mathbf i - \sqrt2\,\mathbf j,
\qquad
\nabla g = (2x)\,\mathbf i + (2y)\,\mathbf j.
</md>
The system <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt2 \amp = 2\lambda x
\;\Rightarrow\; x = \frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda},</mrow>
<mrow>-\sqrt2 \amp = 2\lambda y
\;\Rightarrow\; y = -\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}.</mrow>
</md>
Substituting into the constraint,
<md>
\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}\right)^{2}
+ \left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}\right)^{2} = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; \frac{1}{\lambda^2} = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; \lambda = \pm\frac12.
</md>
For <m>\lambda_1 = \frac12</m>: <m>x = \sqrt2</m>,
<m>y = -\sqrt2</m>. For <m>\lambda_2 = -\frac12</m>:
<m>x = -\sqrt2</m>, <m>y = \sqrt2</m>. Evaluating <m>f</m>,
<md>
<mrow>f\left(\sqrt2, -\sqrt2\right) \amp = 2 + 2 = 4
\qquad \text{absolute maximum,}</mrow>
<mrow>f\left(-\sqrt2, \sqrt2\right) \amp = -2 - 2 = -4
\qquad \text{absolute minimum.}</mrow>
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-rs19-lagrange"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-lagrange">
<caption>
The constraint circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> and the level lines
<m>\sqrt2\,x - \sqrt2\,y = \pm 4</m> of <m>f</m>, which are tangent to
the circle at the extrema
<m>\left(\sqrt2, -\sqrt2\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-\sqrt2, \sqrt2\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rs19-lagrange">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.6,-3.6,3.6,3.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="lg-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<line at="lg-max" endpoints="((-0.4,-3.25),(3.25,0.4))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="lg-min" endpoints="((-3.25,-0.4),(0.4,3.25))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="lg-pmax" p="(1.4142,-1.4142)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="lg-pmin" p="(-1.4142,1.4142)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="lg-pmax-lab" anchor="(1.55,-1.6)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(\sqrt2,-\sqrt2\right)</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-pmin-lab" anchor="(-1.55,1.6)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\left(-\sqrt2,\sqrt2\right)</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-max-lab" anchor="(2.65,-0.05)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=4</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-min-lab" anchor="(-2.65,0.05)" alignment="sw" color="#009E73">
<m>f=-4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius two centered at the origin. A dashed vermillion line of slope one touches the circle at the point square root of two comma negative square root of two, where f equals four, and a dashed green line of slope one touches it at negative square root of two comma square root of two, where f equals negative four.">
  <annotation ref="lg-circle" text="The constraint circle x squared plus y squared equals four."/>
  <annotation ref="lg-pmax" text="The absolute maximum at square root of two comma negative square root of two, where f equals four."/>
  <annotation ref="lg-pmin" text="The absolute minimum at negative square root of two comma square root of two, where f equals negative four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find the point <m>P(x,y,z)</m> closest to the origin on the plane
<m>2x + y - z - 5 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The distance from the origin is <m>\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}</m>, so it
suffices to minimize the function
<m>f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m> subject to the constraint
<m>g(x,y,z) = 2x + y - z - 5 = 0</m>. We compute
<md>
\nabla f = \langle 2x, 2y, 2z\rangle,
\qquad
\nabla g = \langle 2, 1, -1\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The Lagrange system <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>2x \amp = 2\lambda \amp \;\Rightarrow\; x \amp = \lambda,</mrow>
<mrow>2y \amp = \lambda \amp \;\Rightarrow\; y \amp = \frac{\lambda}{2},</mrow>
<mrow>2z \amp = -\lambda \amp \;\Rightarrow\; z \amp = -\frac{\lambda}{2}.</mrow>
</md>
Substituting into the equation of the plane,
<md>
2\lambda + \frac{\lambda}{2} + \frac{\lambda}{2} - 5 = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; 3\lambda = 5
\;\Rightarrow\; \lambda = \frac53.
</md>
Hence the closest point is
<md>
P\left(\frac53,\; \frac56,\; -\frac56\right).
</md>
</p>

<p>
(Only one critical point arises, and the distance certainly has a
minimum on the plane but no maximum, so this point must be the
minimizer.)
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-37">
<statement>
<p>
Given <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 + 4x - y^2 - 4y</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of <m>f(x,y)</m> on the circle
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>, first using Lagrange multipliers, then again by
parametrizing the circle.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of <m>f(x,y)</m> over the disk
<m>x^2 + y^2 \le 9</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<em>Lagrange multipliers.</em> With
<m>g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 9 = 0</m>, the system
<m>\nabla f = \lambda\nabla g</m> reads
<md>
<mrow>-2x + 4 \amp = 2\lambda x \amp \;\Rightarrow\;
x \amp = \frac{2}{1+\lambda},</mrow>
<mrow>-2y - 4 \amp = 2\lambda y \amp \;\Rightarrow\;
y \amp = \frac{-2}{1+\lambda}.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>y = -x</m>, and substituting into the constraint,
<m>2x^2 = 9</m>, so <m>x = \pm\frac{3}{\sqrt2}</m> and
<m>y = \mp\frac{3}{\sqrt2}</m>. Evaluating <m>f</m> at these two
points, and noting that <m>-x^2 - y^2 = -9</m> on the circle,
<md>
f = -9 + 4x - 4y = -9 + 4(x - y) = -9 \pm \frac{24}{\sqrt2}.
</md>
So the maximum on the circle is
<m>-9 + \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m> at
<m>\left(\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m> and the
minimum is <m>-9 - \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m> at
<m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>By parametrizing.</em> On the circle,
<m>x = 3\cos t</m>, <m>y = 3\sin t</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>f \amp = -9\cos^2 t + 12\cos t - 9\sin^2 t - 12\sin t</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -9 + 12(\cos t - \sin t)
= -9 + 12\sqrt2 \cos\!\left(t + \frac{\pi}{4}\right).</mrow>
</md>
Since <m>\cos</m> ranges over <m>[-1,1]</m>, this ranges over
<m>-9 \pm 12\sqrt2 = -9 \pm \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m>, agreeing with the
Lagrange computation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Over the disk we must also check the interior. Setting
<md>
f_x = -2x + 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 2,
\qquad
f_y = -2y - 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = -2,
</md>
gives the critical point <m>(2,-2)</m>, which does lie inside the disk
since <m>4 + 4 = 8 \lt 9</m>. There
<m>f(2,-2) = -4 + 8 - 4 + 8 = 8</m>. (It is a local maximum:
<m>f_{xx} = -2 \lt 0</m>, <m>f_{yy} = -2</m>, <m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>D = 4 \gt 0</m>.)
</p>
<p>
Comparing with the boundary values from part A, the maximum over the
disk is <m>8</m> at <m>(2,-2)</m>, and the minimum is
<m>-9 - \frac{24}{\sqrt2} \approx -25.97</m> at
<m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-disk"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-disk">
<caption>
The disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 9</m>. The maximum of <m>f</m> occurs at the
interior critical point <m>(2,-2)</m>, and the minimum at the boundary
point <m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-disk">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (3*cos(t), 3*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.4,-5.0,5.4,5.0)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="dk2-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dk2-max" p="(2,-2)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dk2-min" p="(-2.1213,2.1213)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="dk2-max-lab" anchor="(2.2,-2.2)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>(2,-2),\ f=8</m>
</label>
<label at="dk2-min-lab" anchor="(-2.3,2.35)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>f=-9-\frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius three centered at the origin. A vermillion point inside the circle at two comma negative two marks the maximum, f equals eight. A green point on the circle in the second quadrant marks the minimum.">
  <annotation ref="dk2-circle" text="The boundary circle x squared plus y squared equals nine."/>
  <annotation ref="dk2-max" text="The interior critical point two comma negative two, where the maximum eight occurs."/>
  <annotation ref="dk2-min" text="The boundary point where the minimum occurs."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-38">
<statement>
<p>
Find the minimum value of <m>x + y^2 + z^3</m> along the part of the
plane <m>x + y + z = 2</m> in the first octant
(<m>x, y, z \ge 0</m>). The solution found by Lagrange multipliers
gives the minimum. Use common sense for the maximum value.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y,z) = x + y^2 + z^3</m> and
<m>g(x,y,z) = x + y + z - 2 = 0</m>. Then
<md>
\nabla f = \left\langle 1, 2y, 3z^2\right\rangle,
\qquad
\nabla g = \langle 1, 1, 1\rangle,
</md>
and <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> gives
<md>
1 = \lambda, \qquad 2y = \lambda, \qquad 3z^2 = \lambda.
</md>
Hence <m>\lambda = 1</m>, <m>y = \frac12</m>, and
<m>z = \frac{1}{\sqrt3}</m> (taking the positive root, since
<m>z \ge 0</m>). From the constraint,
<m>x = 2 - \frac12 - \frac{1}{\sqrt3} = \frac32 - \frac{1}{\sqrt3}</m>,
which is positive, so the point is in the first octant.
</p>

<p>
The minimum value is therefore
<md>
<mrow>f \amp = \left(\frac32 - \frac{1}{\sqrt3}\right)
+ \frac14 + \frac{1}{3\sqrt3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac74 - \frac{1}{\sqrt3} + \frac{1}{3\sqrt3}
= \frac74 - \frac{3}{3\sqrt3} + \frac{1}{3\sqrt3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac74 - \frac{2}{3\sqrt3}
= \frac74 - \frac23\sqrt{\frac13}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
For the maximum, note that the region is a triangle with vertices
<m>(2,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,2,0)</m>, and <m>(0,0,2)</m>. Since the largest
power dominates, the largest value should occur at the vertex where
all the <q>weight</q> is on <m>z</m>. Checking the three vertices,
<md>
f(2,0,0) = 2, \qquad f(0,2,0) = 4, \qquad f(0,0,2) = 8,
</md>
so the maximum value is <m>8</m>, attained at <m>(0,0,2)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-39">
<statement>
<p>
You are in charge of erecting a radio telescope on a newly discovered
planet. To minimize interference, you want to place it where the
magnetic field of the planet is weakest. The planet is spherical, with
a radius of 6 units. Based on a coordinate system whose origin is at
the center of the planet, the strength of the magnetic field is given
by
<md>
M(x,y,z) = 6x - y^2 + xz + 60.
</md>
Where should you locate the radio telescope?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We must minimize <m>M</m> subject to the constraint that we stay on
the surface of the planet, <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 36</m>. The
Lagrange system <m>\nabla M = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>6 + z \amp = 2\lambda x,</mrow>
<mrow>-2y \amp = 2\lambda y,</mrow>
<mrow>x \amp = 2\lambda z.</mrow>
</md>
The second equation factors as <m>2y(\lambda + 1) = 0</m>, so either
<m>\lambda = -1</m> or <m>y = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Case <m>\lambda = -1</m>.</em> The first and third equations
become <m>6 + z = -2x</m> and <m>x = -2z</m>. Substituting,
<m>6 + z = 4z</m>, so <m>z = 2</m> and <m>x = -4</m>. The constraint
then gives <m>16 + y^2 + 4 = 36</m>, i.e. <m>y = \pm 4</m>. At these
points
<md>
M(-4, \pm 4, 2) = -24 - 16 - 8 + 60 = 12.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Case <m>y = 0</m>.</em> From the third equation
<m>\lambda = \dfrac{x}{2z}</m> (with <m>z \ne 0</m>; if <m>z = 0</m>
then <m>x = 0</m> and the first equation gives <m>6 = 0</m>, which is
impossible). Substituting into the first,
<m>6 + z = \dfrac{x^2}{z}</m>, i.e. <m>x^2 = 6z + z^2</m>. Combined
with <m>x^2 + z^2 = 36</m> this gives
<md>
6z + z^2 = 36 - z^2
\;\Rightarrow\; 2z^2 + 6z - 36 = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; (z+6)(z-3) = 0,
</md>
so <m>z = -6</m> (giving <m>x = 0</m>, with <m>M = 60</m>) or
<m>z = 3</m> (giving <m>x = \pm 3\sqrt3</m>, with
<m>M = 60 \pm 27\sqrt3 \approx 13.2</m> or <m>106.8</m>).
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the candidate values
<m>\{12,\; 60,\; 13.2,\; 106.8\}</m>, the magnetic field is weakest
where <m>M = 12</m>. So the radio telescope should be located at
<md>
(-4, 4, 2) \qquad\text{or}\qquad (-4, -4, 2).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
